1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,219
On the night of the eighteenth of July, AD 64, a fire broke out among the shops beside the Circus Maximus, Rome's great chariot-racing track.

2
00:00:12,319 --> 00:00:19,849
Driven by the wind, it burned for six days. The crowded wooden tenements of the city went up street by street.

3
00:00:20,949 --> 00:00:30,037
According to the historian Tacitus, of the city's fourteen districts, three were completely destroyed and seven more were left in ruins.

4
00:00:31,137 --> 00:00:40,737
The emperor, Nero, has been blamed for it ever since. The story says he fiddled while Rome burned. But the fiddle had not been invented yet.

5
00:00:41,837 --> 00:00:57,944
Almost everything we know about Nero comes from three writers: Tacitus, Suetonius and Cassius Dio. All wrote decades or more after his death, and all shared the outlook of Rome's governing class, which hated him. Keep that in mind.

6
00:01:00,144 --> 00:01:11,600
Nero was born on the fifteenth of December, AD 37, at Antium, on the coast south of Rome. His birth name was Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus.

7
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:21,916
His mother, Agrippina the Younger, was a great-granddaughter of Rome's first emperor, Augustus, and the sister of the emperor Caligula.

8
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:33,427
When Nero was a toddler, Caligula sent Agrippina into exile, and soon afterwards Nero's father died. For a time, the boy was raised by an aunt.

9
00:01:34,527 --> 00:01:46,729
Agrippina was recalled under the next emperor, Claudius. In AD 49 she married him, though he was her own uncle, and persuaded him to adopt her son, who took the name Nero.

10
00:01:47,829 --> 00:01:58,005
Claudius already had a younger son, Britannicus. Agrippina made sure Nero was promoted ahead of him, and married him to Claudius's daughter, Octavia.

11
00:02:00,205 --> 00:02:12,941
In October AD 54, Claudius died suddenly. The ancient historians say Agrippina poisoned him with a dish of mushrooms. Modern historians think it possible, but cannot be sure.

12
00:02:14,041 --> 00:02:18,756
Nero became emperor at sixteen, the youngest Rome had yet seen.

13
00:02:19,856 --> 00:02:31,483
His old tutor, the philosopher Seneca, and the commander of the guard, Burrus, helped him govern. Even later critics remembered his first years as a time of sound government.

14
00:02:32,583 --> 00:02:40,753
He staged lavish games, and once even proposed abolishing some taxes. With ordinary Romans, he was popular.

15
00:02:41,853 --> 00:02:51,795
His reign also saw a great revolt in the new province of Britain, led by Queen Boudica. Roman towns were burned before the legions crushed it.

16
00:02:53,995 --> 00:03:03,232
In AD 55, Britannicus collapsed at a palace dinner and died. The sources say Nero had him poisoned. He was not yet fourteen.

17
00:03:04,332 --> 00:03:16,812
Agrippina had expected to rule through her son. As he pulled away from her, the relationship turned bitter. According to the sources, Nero first tried to drown her in a boat built to collapse.

18
00:03:17,912 --> 00:03:27,853
She swam to safety. Soldiers were sent to her villa to finish the job. On this, the ancient writers all agree: Nero had his own mother killed.

19
00:03:28,953 --> 00:03:38,980
In AD 62, he divorced his wife, Octavia, exiled her to a small island, and had her put to death. He then married Poppaea Sabina.

20
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:48,443
That same year, Burrus died and Seneca withdrew from public life. Without them, Nero ruled more and more as he pleased.

21
00:03:50,643 --> 00:03:58,493
Two years later came the Great Fire. When it began, Nero was at his villa in Antium. He hurried back to the city.

22
00:03:59,593 --> 00:04:08,212
According to Tacitus, he opened his own gardens and public buildings to the homeless, brought in food, and cut the price of grain.

23
00:04:09,312 --> 00:04:21,579
But a rumour spread that while the city burned, he had sung on a private stage about the destruction of Troy. Tacitus reports it only as a rumour. Later writers treated it as fact.

24
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:32,855
What came next made things worse. On land cleared by the fire, Nero began a vast new palace, the Golden House, with gardens and an artificial lake.

25
00:04:33,955 --> 00:04:42,979
So did he start the fire? Most modern historians think not. Fires were common in Rome, and this one also destroyed his own palace.

26
00:04:45,179 --> 00:04:53,861
Nero needed someone to blame. According to Tacitus, he chose the Christians, then a small and distrusted sect in the city.

27
00:04:54,961 --> 00:05:06,460
Many were arrested and put to death in cruel public spectacles. Christian tradition holds that the apostles Peter and Paul were also killed in Rome under Nero.

28
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:19,113
Nero's real passion was performance. He sang, played the lyre and drove chariots in public, which shocked Rome's upper class, who looked down on performers.

29
00:05:20,213 --> 00:05:34,485
In AD 65, a plot to kill him was uncovered, named after a senator, Gaius Calpurnius Piso. Many were executed or ordered to kill themselves, including Seneca and his nephew, the poet Lucan.

30
00:05:35,585 --> 00:05:45,377
That year, Poppaea died while pregnant. The ancient writers claim he kicked her in a rage. Some modern historians suspect illness, or a miscarriage.

31
00:05:46,477 --> 00:05:59,341
In AD 66, he left for a long tour of Greece, competing at its great festivals. Suetonius says he fell from his chariot at Olympia, but the judges declared him the winner anyway.

32
00:06:00,441 --> 00:06:11,748
Meanwhile the empire was under strain. In AD 66, a major Jewish revolt broke out in Judaea, and Nero sent the general Vespasian to put it down.

33
00:06:12,848 --> 00:06:22,448
Back in Rome, money was running short. Confiscations and heavy demands angered the provinces, and the army's commanders were losing patience.

34
00:06:24,648 --> 00:06:36,552
In AD 68, Gaius Julius Vindex, a governor in Gaul, rebelled, and Galba, the governor in Spain, joined him. Vindex was soon defeated, but the revolt spread.

35
00:06:37,652 --> 00:06:43,348
The palace guard abandoned Nero, and the Senate declared him an enemy of the state.

36
00:06:44,448 --> 00:06:56,139
He fled to a freedman's villa outside the city. On the ninth of June, AD 68, as horsemen approached, he took his own life, with help from his secretary. He was thirty.

37
00:06:57,239 --> 00:07:02,252
According to Suetonius, his last words were: what an artist dies in me.

38
00:07:03,352 --> 00:07:13,315
His death ended the dynasty of Augustus. Within a year, four men had claimed the throne, in a civil war known as the Year of the Four Emperors.

39
00:07:15,515 --> 00:07:28,485
Not everyone celebrated. Suetonius records that for a long time people laid flowers on his tomb. Several impostors later claimed to be Nero returned, and found followers in the east.

40
00:07:29,585 --> 00:07:38,716
Many scholars believe that the number of the beast in the Book of Revelation, six hundred and sixty-six, is a coded reference to Nero.

41
00:07:39,816 --> 00:07:49,928
Some things about Nero are not in doubt. He had his mother and his first wife killed, he persecuted Christians, and he had critics and rivals put to death.

42
00:07:51,028 --> 00:08:03,743
But modern historians, such as Edward Champlin, point out that the elite writers who told his story had every reason to make him a monster, and that many ordinary Romans seem to have liked him.

43
00:08:04,843 --> 00:08:16,704
Nero was vain and murderous. But he did not fiddle while Rome burned. The monster of the legend was written by his enemies. The real man was bad enough, and more complicated.
